FINAL ANSWER:
Top five by surviving Go lines, counted with `git blame -w -M -C -C` at HEAD. That is 77 Go files and 14,434 lines attributed, out of 301 non-merge commits.

| # | Author | Surviving lines | Share | Commits (share) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Eric Paris | 2,608 | 18.1% | 46 (15.3%) |
| 2 | Alex Ogier | 1,512 | 10.5% | 33 (11.0%) |
| 3 | Marcus Lindqvist | 1,020 | 7.1% | 3 (1.0%) |
| 4 | Jonathan Reyna | 950 | 6.6% | 1 (0.3%) |
| 5 | Noah Dietz | 919 | 6.4% | 2 (0.7%) |

- **Marcus Lindqvist is a guess.** His 1,020 lines come from three addresses that I counted as one person:
  - `<email redacted>` has 634 lines.
  - `mlindqvist@corp.example` has 322 lines.
  - `<email redacted>` has 64 lines.
  - The tool matched them on name and on his GitHub login appearing as a name. Nobody has confirmed it.
  - Unmerged, his biggest address (634 lines, 4.4%) would rank below Tamal Saha's 641 lines. He would then drop out of the top five, and Tamal Saha (641 lines, 4.4%) would replace Jonathan Reyna or Noah Dietz only if the merge were wrong and the others also shifted. The top two are the same either way.
- **Concentration is moderate.** The bus factor is 6. That is the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines together reach 50% of the lines counted (53.0% here). It is computed on Go files only. Reaching 80% takes 19 authors.
- **Commit counts and line counts disagree for some people.** Jonathan Reyna, Noah Dietz and Marcus Lindqvist each hold many lines from 1–3 commits, so they likely landed large chunks. Eric Paris and Alex Ogier are the only top-five authors with a steady commit history. I didn't inspect those commits.
- **Left out of the numbers:**
  - One vendored file (802 lines).
  - One bot identity (77 lines, 1 commit).
  - No reformat commit was detected, so none was ignored.
- **Other possible duplicates.** The tool suggested some other same-person groups, such as Mitch Connors/`therealmitchconnors` (500 lines under the login) and Albert Nigmatzianov/`bogem`. They don't change the top five, because even doubled they stay below 919 lines. I did not write a `.mailmap`, since you only asked a question.
- **What the numbers don't show.** Surviving lines say nothing about code quality, code review, design work or code that was deleted on purpose. They show whose code is still here, not who would be the best maintainer. Check recent activity as well before choosing. I did not check last-commit dates, and `--reviewers` can show them.

